--TEST--
`Core\Response::text` writes its argument to the request's output with nothing escaped or substituted, because the media type it declares is not HTML -- ADR 0088 §§ 3-4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/response-body-is-one-typed-member`: `text` owns one body shape and sets `text/plain` for it, so
// the bytes are the argument's own. Markup in the argument is markup in the
// body -- an escape here would be the HTML sink's rule applied to a body that
// declared it is not HTML.
Core\Response::text("<b>a & b</b>");
echo "\n";

// § 3's table: the bytes go to whatever sink is in force, which is what makes
// the member spellable at all outside a request. The member names the shape;
// the context names the destination.
Core\Response::text("");
Core\Response::text("plain");
echo "\n";
--EXPECT--
<b>a & b</b>
plain
